### How does mapping work as a downstream collector?

```java
package com.bank.analytics;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class MappingCollectorDemo {
    record Account(String id, String holder, String branch, BigDecimal balance) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Account> accounts = List.of(
            new Account("SAV2001", "Rajesh", "MUMBAI",  new BigDecimal("50000")),
            new Account("SAV2002", "Priya",  "MUMBAI",  new BigDecimal("150000")),
            new Account("SAV2003", "Amit",   "DELHI",   new BigDecimal("75000")),
            new Account("SAV2004", "Kavita", "DELHI",   new BigDecimal("120000")),
            new Account("SAV2005", "Suresh", "CHENNAI", new BigDecimal("200000"))
        );

        // Without mapping: Map<String, List<Account>>
        Map<String, List<Account>> byBranch = accounts.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Account::branch));

        // With mapping: Map<String, List<String>> (just holder names)
        Map<String, List<String>> holdersByBranch = accounts.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                Account::branch,
                Collectors.mapping(Account::holder, Collectors.toList())));

        System.out.println(holdersByBranch);
    }
}
```

```text
{MUMBAI=[Rajesh, Priya], DELHI=[Amit, Kavita], CHENNAI=[Suresh]}
```

### How do you use mapping with other downstream collectors?

```java
// mapping + joining: comma-separated holder names per branch
Map<String, String> holdersCsv = accounts.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
        Account::branch,
        Collectors.mapping(Account::holder, Collectors.joining(", "))));
// {MUMBAI=Rajesh, Priya, DELHI=Amit, Kavita, CHENNAI=Suresh}

// mapping + toSet: unique account IDs per branch
Map<String, Set<String>> idsByBranch = accounts.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
        Account::branch,
        Collectors.mapping(Account::id, Collectors.toSet())));

// mapping + reducing: sum of balances per branch
Map<String, BigDecimal> totalByBranch = accounts.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
        Account::branch,
        Collectors.mapping(Account::balance,
            Collectors.reducing(BigDecimal.ZERO, BigDecimal::add))));
```

### How is mapping different from map() on the stream?

| Feature | `stream.map()` | `Collectors.mapping()` |
|---------|---------------|----------------------|
| Type | Intermediate operation | Downstream collector |
| Scope | Entire stream | Within each group |
| Use with | Any stream operation | groupingBy, partitioningBy |

`stream.map()` transforms before grouping. `Collectors.mapping()` transforms after grouping, within each group.

### What are common mistakes?

WRONG: Using `stream.map()` before groupingBy when you want to transform within groups.

```java
// WRONG: map before groupBy loses the original department info
accounts.stream()
    .map(Account::holder)  // now Stream<String>, no department!
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(???)); // cannot group by department
```

CORRECT: Use mapping as a downstream.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- `mapping` with `reducing` achieves per-group aggregation on projected fields in a single collect call.
- `flatMapping` (Java 9) is the downstream equivalent of `flatMap()`: it flattens a nested collection within each group.
- For simple extraction, consider whether `Collectors.toMap` with a value mapper might be simpler than `groupingBy` with `mapping`.
